In simple business terms

You can think of the flow as:

1. Identify an object/instrument in SensI
↓
2. If there is an instrument-related problem → Instrument Exception
↓
3. That exception can be associated with a pricing-related problem → Pricing Exception
↑
4. Market-data-related problems can also be associated with the pricing exception

And separately:

Instrument → CAIRA Instrument ID → ISIN / Bloomberg Code / RIC / Sophis ID / etc.

Important observation

The Instrument ID Mapping table does not appear to have a relationship line drawn to the other four tables in the screenshot. So I would not assume a database FK relationship between it and the exception tables based on this image alone.

Also, the arrows in an ERD can mean different things depending on the notation/tool used. From this screenshot I can confidently identify the entities and fields, but I would avoid claiming that the arrows represent a specific FK direction without seeing the actual schema/relationship definitions.

If you're using this for your trade-reporting / exception-handling architecture, the practical interpretation is essentially:

Object/Instrument → Instrument Exception → Pricing Exception ← Market Data Exception, with Instrument ID Mapping providing the cross-system instrument identifiers.
